# Shell scripts must keep LF endings even when checked out on Windows.
# bash reads a CRLF script literally: `set -euo pipefail\r` parses as the
# option name "pipefail\r", which fails with "invalid option name" and
# takes the whole build down. This only surfaced on `vercel --prod` from a
# Windows machine (which uploads the local working copy); git-triggered
# builds were fine because the repo itself stores LF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# SQL migrations are pasted into Supabase's SQL editor and read on Linux
# hosts; no reason to carry CRLF into either.
*.sql text eol=lf

# The agent team's vendored sources and its two rendered editions must be LF
# everywhere. `.ai/agent-team/build.py` always writes LF, and CI runs
# `omni agents sync --check` as a blocking step (see .github/workflows/ci.yml).
# Left to core.autocrlf these check out as CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux, so
# the same tree would pass the gate on one platform and fail on the other, and
# every sync would rewrite files it had just verified.
# Forced `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto`: if a skill ever ships a binary
# reference (an image or PDF under skills/*/references/), add a `-text` rule
# for it here, or checkout will corrupt it.
.ai/agent-team/** text eol=lf
.claude/agents/** text eol=lf
.claude/skills/** text eol=lf
managed-agents/agents/** text eol=lf
